.PP
The process table contains the following columns:
.TP
.B PID
Process ID, as listed under /proc.
.TP
.B USER
Effective user name, resolved from /etc/passwd; falls back to the
numeric uid if the user is not known. A trailing \fB+\fR marks a name
truncated to the eight-character USER field.
.TP
.B PR
Scheduling priority. "rt" indicates a real-time priority.
.TP
.B NI
Nice value. Higher values lower the process's scheduling priority.
.TP
.B VIRT
Virtual memory size of the process. Batch output keeps KiB integers
that fit their fields: seven columns for VIRT, six for RES and SHR.
Larger values use binary \fBm\fR/\fBg\fR/\fBt\fR/\fBp\fR suffixes
with one decimal place.
.TP
.B RES
Resident set size — non-swapped physical memory in use.
.TP
.B SHR
Resident shared memory.
.TP
.B S
Process state: \fBR\fR (running), \fBS\fR (sleeping), \fBD\fR
(uninterruptible), \fBT\fR (stopped), \fBZ\fR (zombie), or \fBI\fR
(idle).
.TP
.B %CPU
Cumulative CPU usage since the process started, expressed as a
percentage of one CPU's wall-clock time.
.TP
.B %MEM
Resident set size as a percentage of total physical memory.
.TP
.B TIME+
Total CPU time consumed by the process, in MM:SS.cc format.
.TP
.B COMMAND
Command name (default) or full command line (toggle with \fBc\fR).
